<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bautzen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bautzen is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> and the cultural centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>. It led the Lusatian League of six towns founded in 1346, and its cathedral of St Peter has been shared by Catholics and Lutherans since the 1520s. In 1813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> won a battle there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bautzen.jpg" alt="Bautzen" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan-Herm Janßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bautzen_Altstadt_57.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bautzen, known in Upper Sorbian as Budyšin, lies on the River Spree about 50 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in the bilingual Sorbian settlement area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>. The old town stands on a plateau above the river, topped by Ortenburg Castle and bordered by the city walls. Until 1868 its German name was Budissin, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> government changed it to Bautzen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An eastern Germanic settlement existed here in the 3rd century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a> arrived in the 6th. The first written mention, as Budusin, dates from 1002. In 1018 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-bautzen">Peace of Bautzen</a> between the German king Henry II and the Polish ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a> left the town under Polish rule. In 1032 it passed to the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, in 1075 to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, which became a kingdom in 1198.</p><p>In 1346 the town joined the Lusatian League with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamenz">Kamenz</a>, Lauban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zittau">Zittau</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> besieged it unsuccessfully in 1429 and 1431. In 1547 Ferdinand I, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>, imposed the Upper Lusatian Pönfall on it after disobedience in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>, and the town lost its self-governing privileges. The Swedes destroyed it in 1634 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and in 1635 it fell to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p>The Battle of Bautzen was fought here in 1813 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. Sorbian life centred on the town in the 19th century: the student society Societas Slavica Budissenensis was founded in 1839, the Sorbian national anthem was first performed in public in 1845, and the Sorbian House opened in 1904. Under the Nazi regime political prisoners were held in the prisons Bautzen I and II, and a subcamp of the Groß-Rosen concentration camp operated here. Bautzen II held political prisoners in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and has been a memorial since 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bautzen had about 38,000 inhabitants in 2021. It is the political and cultural centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, with the Domowina, Sorbian Radio and the Sorbian Institute, and about 10 percent of its people speak Upper Sorbian. The medieval centre keeps its walls, the leaning Reichenturm, the Old Waterworks of 1558 and St Peter's Cathedral, the only historic interdenominational church building in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bautzen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Third Silesian War, an Austrian army of 80,000 under Leopold Josef Graf Daun surprised Frederick the Great's Prussian army of 30,000–36,000 at Hochkirch, near <b>Bautzen</b> in Saxony, and forced a general retreat.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-14">14 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1346 <b>Bautzen</b>, Görlitz, Zittau, Kamenz, Löbau and Lauban, towns of the Margraviate of Upper Lusatia, then a land of the Kingdom of Bohemia, formed a league to suppress robber knights and protect their trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusatian-league">Lusatian League</a></li><li><blockquote>The area around <b>Bautzen</b>, the land of the Slavic Milceni, belonged to the Margraviate of Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li><li><blockquote>A Catholic remnant in Upper Lusatia continued at <b>Bautzen</b> and became the modern Diocese of Dresden-Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-meissen">Prince-Bishopric of Meissen</a></li></ul></section>
